---
/**
 * /cedar — the Cedar product page. This page owns the AI story so
 * the homepage, pricing and methodology stop re-explaining it. One
 * argument: Cedar was designed into the economic-analysis workflow
 * from the beginning, and Lumecon's implementation is different
 * because the model, data, software and AI are developed together.
 *
 * Visuals: three real product captures telling one story in order
 * (upload, operations with Cedar's ask, board notes). No diagrams,
 * no vendor logos, no chat-bubble clichés.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import MarkArt from '../components/MarkArt.astro';
import { site } from '../config';
import '../styles/global.css';
import '../styles/nav.css';
import '../styles/footer.css';
import '../styles/home.css';
import '../styles/methodology.css';
import '../styles/tour.css';
import '../styles/cedar-page.css';

const description =
  'Cedar is Lumecon’s AI economic analyst, designed into the workflow from the beginning: reading source documents, structuring model-ready inputs, surfacing gaps, supporting interpretation and helping communicate findings. AI in the workflow, economists in the loop.';

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'WebPage',
    name: 'Cedar | Lumecon',
    url: 'https://lumecon.ai/cedar',
    description,
    isPartOf: { '@id': `${site.url}#website` },
    publisher: { '@id': `${site.url}#org` },
    inLanguage: 'en-US',
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: site.url },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Cedar', item: 'https://lumecon.ai/cedar' },
    ],
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Cedar, the AI economic analyst | Lumecon"
  description={description}
  canonical="https://lumecon.ai/cedar"
  keywords="Cedar AI, AI economic analyst, AI economic impact analysis, AI for economic modeling, document ingestion economic analysis, AI assisted input-output modeling, Lumecon Cedar"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <Nav isHome={false} />
  <main class="meth cedarpg" id="top" tabindex="-1">
    <!-- Hero -->
    <header class="meth-hero">
      <MarkArt class="markart markart--tr" size={620} />
      <div class="meth-w">
        <p class="h-kicker reveal-soft">Cedar</p>
        <h1 class="meth-hero__title reveal-soft" data-stagger="1">
          AI built for economic analysis <span class="brand-em">from the beginning.</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="meth-hero__lede reveal-soft" data-stagger="2">
          Cedar is Lumecon&rsquo;s AI economic analyst. It works across intake, analysis,
          interpretation and reporting, helping turn organizational knowledge and source documents
          into usable economic analysis. <span class="brand-em"
            >It is part of how Lumecon works, present from intake through reporting.</span
          >
        </p>
        <div class="cedarpg-cta reveal-soft" data-stagger="3">
          <a class="btn2 btn2--primary" href="/signup?tier=free">
            Try Lumecon <span class="btn2-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- Three acts, one screenshot each, in the order the work happens:
         Cedar reads what you already have, it does the analysis with you,
         and it carries your organization's context between projects.

         Built on the homepage tour's own row: text one side, screenshot the
         other, alternating, sliding in from opposite edges. Stacking a
         full-width screenshot under a paragraph, which is what this was,
         gives the reveal nothing to play against and puts the picture a
         scroll away from the words it illustrates.

         This replaced nine sections that argued the same three points from
         different angles: "AI shouldn't sit on top of the model" and
         "economics and engineering are developed together" were one claim
         told twice, and "built to use the best tools available" was a
         34-word section with its own heading. The how-it-is-built material
         is a three-item band at the end rather than three more sections. -->

    <section class="tour cedarpg-acts" aria-label="How Cedar works">
      <div class="tour-inner tour-rows">
        <article class="tour-row" aria-labelledby="c-analyst">
          <div class="tour-row__text reveal-left">
            <h2 class="tour-row__title" id="c-analyst">Cedar is an economic analyst.</h2>
            <p class="tour-row__body">
              Most assistants are bolted onto finished software and answer questions once the
              results are out. Cedar works through the whole analysis and starts where your work
              already is: upload PDFs, spreadsheets and prior analyses, and it maps what matters
              into the model. <span class="brand-em"
                >It flags what it cannot resolve rather than papering over it.</span
              >
            </p>
          </div>
          <figure class="tour-row__shot reveal-right">
            <img
              src="/app/cedar-wind-upload.webp"
              alt="The first step of a new analysis: two PDFs, a capital plan and county road-use agreements, uploading to Cedar with live per-file status, alongside a note telling Cedar to watch for the substation electrical line item."
              width="1920"
              height="1080"
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
            />
            <figcaption class="cedarpg-cap">
              The wind project&rsquo;s source documents uploading to Cedar, with a note about what
              to watch for. Shown with sample data.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        </article>
        <article class="tour-row tour-row--flip" aria-labelledby="c-work">
          <div class="tour-row__text reveal-right">
            <h2 class="tour-row__title" id="c-work">It does the analysis with you.</h2>
            <p class="tour-row__body">
              Those documents become operations the model can run, each carded with its industry.
              Cedar suggests, questions, organizes and drafts, and explains one finding differently
              for a council, a grantmaker or a board. Every assumption it makes surfaces for the
              analyst to confirm before results are final. <span class="brand-em"
                >AI in the workflow. Economists in the loop.</span
              >
            </p>
          </div>
          <figure class="tour-row__shot reveal-left">
            <img
              src="/app/cedar-wind-entities.webp"
              alt="The operations step of the wind analysis: three entity cards with their sector icons, turbine and storage installation, grid interconnection and operations and maintenance, beside the open Cedar panel reporting that two operations are model ready and asking to fill the missing sales figure from the procurement workbook."
              width="1920"
              height="1080"
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
            />
            <figcaption class="cedarpg-cap">
              The same documents turned into operations. Cedar reports what is model ready and asks
              about the one figure still missing. Shown with sample data.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        </article>
        <article class="tour-row" aria-labelledby="c-org">
          <div class="tour-row__text reveal-left">
            <h2 class="tour-row__title" id="c-org">
              It works from your organization&rsquo;s context.
            </h2>
            <p class="tour-row__body">
              Cedar carries approved organizational context between projects, so teams build on
              prior work instead of starting from zero, and larger organizations can calibrate it
              around their terminology and reporting. Cedar Commons is where that work lives: one
              board a team returns to, each project with its own collaborators, notes and a Cedar
              that understands it. <span class="brand-em"
                >Partners get one project and nothing more.</span
              >
            </p>
          </div>
          <figure class="tour-row__shot reveal-right">
            <img
              src="/app/cedar-context.webp"
              alt="The Cedar Commons board with the Cedar panel open on a shared project, answering 'What changed since my last visit?' with who re-ran the analysis, how the jobs and output figures moved, and the note an external reviewer left, scoped to that project."
              width="1920"
              height="1080"
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
            />
            <figcaption class="cedarpg-cap">
              Cedar answers from inside any project on the board: who re-ran what, how the figures
              moved, what a reviewer flagged. Shown with sample data.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- How it is built. Three short answers to the questions the three acts
         raise, as a band rather than three more sections with headings.

         Each answer holds its sentence back until the pointer arrives, the
         same .hover-veil pattern the homepage edge tiles use, so the band
         reads as three labels until you want the detail. -->
    <section class="meth-sec cedarpg-band" aria-labelledby="c-built">
      <div class="meth-w">
        <div class="meth-sec__head">
          <h2 class="reveal-soft" id="c-built">How it is built.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="cedarpg-facts">
          <div class="cedarpg-fact reveal-soft" data-stagger="1" data-hover-reveal tabindex="0">
            <p class="h-kicker">Developed together</p>
            <h3 class="cedarpg-fact__t">Economics and engineering, one team</h3>
            <p class="hover-veil">
              Lumecon is developed across economics, social science, data science and software
              engineering, where modeling decisions shape the software and new data changes what can
              be estimated. The model, data pipeline, software and AI evolve together, which is why
              Cedar behaves like part of the analysis.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="cedarpg-fact reveal-soft" data-stagger="2" data-hover-reveal tabindex="0">
            <p class="h-kicker">Your work stays yours</p>
            <h3 class="cedarpg-fact__t">Your data is never shared</h3>
            <p class="hover-veil">
              Customer proprietary source material is not made available to other organizations.
              Improvements to Lumecon&rsquo;s processing and modeling can be informed by aggregated
              or appropriately processed information without exposing another customer&rsquo;s
              underlying data. The <a class="inline-link" href="/privacy">privacy policy</a> governs the
              details.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="cedarpg-fact reveal-soft" data-stagger="3" data-hover-reveal tabindex="0">
            <p class="h-kicker">A wider evidence base</p>
            <h3 class="cedarpg-fact__t">More evidence, the same economics</h3>
            <p class="hover-veil">
              Modern AI makes larger and more varied datasets practical to process, harmonize,
              classify and validate, which expands the evidence available to the model. The
              economics stay grounded in the{' '}
              <a class="inline-link" href="/methodology">documented methodology</a>, and
              Cedar&rsquo;s architecture takes advantage of leading AI capabilities as the field
              improves.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="meth-mission" aria-labelledby="c-close">
      <div class="meth-w">
        <h2 class="meth-mission__title" id="c-close">
          Cedar keeps learning <span class="brand-em">alongside the data, models and methods.</span>
        </h2>
        <p class="meth-mission__lede">
          We are continually expanding the formats Cedar can understand, the workflows it can
          support, the context it can use and the ways modern data can strengthen economic analysis.
        </p>
        <div class="meth-mission__cta">
          <a class="btn2 btn2--primary" href="/signup?tier=free">
            Try Lumecon <span class="btn2-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
          </a>
          <a class="btn2 btn2--card" href="/methodology">
            Read the methodology <span class="btn2-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <Footer isHome={false} />
</BaseLayout>
